    "result_text": "Build succeeds with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain email blocklist as a small, single-responsibility step slotted into the existing owner email pipeline, following the repo's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**New files:**\n- `DisposableEmailDomains.java` \u2014 holds the blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`) and the `isDisposable(email)` decision. The blocklist has a single home.\n- `RejectDisposableEmail.java` \u2014 the pipeline step; reads the published `OwnerFieldsDto`, skips null/blank emails (email is optional), and throws `DisposableEmailException` when the domain is blocked.\n- `DisposableEmailException.java` + `DisposableEmailExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 checked escalation \u2192 HTTP 400, mirroring the existing `InvalidEmail` pair.\n- `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.DisposableEmailException.yml` \u2014 wires the handler.\n\n**Edits:**\n- `EmailNormalizer.java` \u2014 added a `domainOf(email)` helper so domain parsing lives with the other shared email logic and the policy class reuses it rather than re-parsing.\n- `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml` \u2014 inserted a `rejectDisposableEmail` step right after `normalizeEmail`, so the check runs on the already-validated, lower-cased address and before any owner is built/loaded or saved.\n\nThe check runs after normalization (so it's case-insensitive and robust), and applies to both create and update for consistency.",
